            <ArticleTitle>Thyroidectomy in high body mass index patients: A single center experience.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>S38-41</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.ijsu.2015.12.054</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S1743-9191(15)01440-5</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="BACKGROUND" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">To identify the correlation between high body mass index (BMI ≥ 25) and the risk of postoperative complications of thyroidectomy.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">A comparative study between thyroidectomy performed in normal or overweigh-obese patients has been performed. Postoperative outcomes, including hypocalcemia, laryngeal nerve palsy, bleeding, operation time and hospital stay, were evaluated.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">A total of 266 patients underwent total thyroidectomy were included. Of them, 104 patients had a BMI below 25 and 162 patients had a BMI ≥ 25. There was no statistically significant difference in the occurrence of early or permanent hypoparathyroidism, recurrent laryngeal nerve palsy, bleeding complications, or postoperative duration of hospital stay. There was, however, a higher operative time in patients with a BMI ≥ 25.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Despite the longer operative time, thyroidectomy can be performed safely in patients with a BMI ≥ 25.</AbstractText>
